WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.422 --> 00:00:02.356
- There are absolutely no shortcuts,

00:00:02.356 --> 00:00:03.286
it takes time.

00:00:03.286 --> 00:00:06.116
If I wasn't paid a lot of money,

00:00:06.116 --> 00:00:09.023
I would still do what I do.

00:00:09.023 --> 00:00:10.785
There isn't enough hours in the day

00:00:10.785 --> 00:00:12.881
to be able to do both extremely well.

00:00:12.881 --> 00:00:16.307
There is nobody on planet Earth like you.

00:00:16.307 --> 00:00:19.649
I set up pretty high goals for myself.

00:00:19.649 --> 00:00:22.486
It takes and element of defining
the ultimate destination,

00:00:22.486 --> 00:00:24.404
then empowering people to actually go.

00:00:24.404 --> 00:00:27.248
Let me apprentice you, let me show you how

00:00:27.248 --> 00:00:28.268
to become good at it.

00:00:28.268 --> 00:00:29.857
I didn't have a clue what I wanted to be.

00:00:29.857 --> 00:00:32.277
You've got to get comfortable
with being uncomfortable.

00:00:32.277 --> 00:00:34.211
But life is funny, you
know, because you get

00:00:34.211 --> 00:00:35.886
the money and you get the knowledge,

00:00:35.886 --> 00:00:37.111
but life has passed you by.

00:00:37.111 --> 00:00:37.953
(swooshes)

00:00:37.953 --> 00:00:39.937
- He's the CEO of Deloitte and Touche,

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:43.371
he was named one of
consulting magazine's top 25

00:00:43.371 --> 00:00:45.413
most influential consultants.

00:00:45.413 --> 00:00:47.760
He speaks regularly with top tier media

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.350
as well as premiere conferences
and business schools.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.382
He's Punit Renjen, and here are his

00:00:52.382 --> 00:00:54.406
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:54.406 --> 00:00:56.656
(swooshes)

00:00:57.536 --> 00:01:01.531
- Well I think there's no
substitute for hard work.

00:01:01.531 --> 00:01:02.689
None, zero.

00:01:02.689 --> 00:01:06.405
I was in India, I talked
to 200 of the newly hired

00:01:06.405 --> 00:01:09.884
individuals and I can
say this because I'm of

00:01:09.884 --> 00:01:12.154
Indian heritage, they always
ask me when I go to India,

00:01:12.154 --> 00:01:15.178
so tell us the one or two shortcuts

00:01:15.178 --> 00:01:16.809
to get to your point.

00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:18.437
And I always say there are no shortcuts,

00:01:18.437 --> 00:01:20.020
you've got to put in the time,

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.700
and so I think the first takeaway is

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.661
there are absolutely no shortcuts.

00:01:24.661 --> 00:01:27.851
It takes time, and it
takes a lot of hard work.

00:01:27.851 --> 00:01:30.514
I mean I really, really enjoy what I do.

00:01:30.514 --> 00:01:33.842
I really do, and I think,
a number of you probably

00:01:33.842 --> 00:01:36.923
are interested in consulting,
I think my only advice

00:01:36.923 --> 00:01:39.843
and I hope that I'm a
few years older than you

00:01:39.843 --> 00:01:42.421
so I have the right to give
you a little bit of advice.

00:01:42.421 --> 00:01:44.620
But my only advice is pick something

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.542
that you will thoroughly enjoy,

00:01:46.542 --> 00:01:47.953
whatever that may be.

00:01:47.953 --> 00:01:51.081
And I think if you do that, you will,

00:01:51.081 --> 00:01:52.835
to answer the first individual's question,

00:01:52.835 --> 00:01:55.934
you'll probably survive
in it, and potentially,

00:01:55.934 --> 00:01:57.547
maybe you'll thrive in it.

00:01:57.547 --> 00:01:58.713
I mean I usually don't say this,

00:01:58.713 --> 00:02:01.713
but if I wasn't paid a lot of money,

00:02:03.336 --> 00:02:06.349
I would still do what I do.

00:02:06.349 --> 00:02:09.983
I hope some of our
managing partners don't see

00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:12.868
this program, because
my units may get cut,

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:15.451
but I would still do what I do.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.743
You know I'm surprised on a yearly basis

00:02:19.743 --> 00:02:21.811
as to what kind of money I make.

00:02:21.811 --> 00:02:23.727
I mean I just am absolutely surprised,

00:02:23.727 --> 00:02:27.346
I would do it for a tenth of what I make.

00:02:27.346 --> 00:02:30.126
So just find something
that you really enjoy,

00:02:30.126 --> 00:02:31.385
and I think you'll thrive.

00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:33.664
I define success two ways.

00:02:33.664 --> 00:02:36.331
One, what I do here at the firm.

00:02:38.177 --> 00:02:40.273
The impact that I create for my clients,

00:02:40.273 --> 00:02:42.363
the impact that I create
for the partnership,

00:02:42.363 --> 00:02:45.479
now the new role that I have,
the legacy that I leave.

00:02:45.479 --> 00:02:48.458
That is one way that
I will define success.

00:02:48.458 --> 00:02:49.899
You know I'm not a great cricketer,

00:02:49.899 --> 00:02:53.072
I love cricket, I wish I was
as good as Sachin Tendulkar,

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:55.884
I'm not, this is my life's work.

00:02:55.884 --> 00:02:59.992
What I do here is my life's
work, it will define me.

00:02:59.992 --> 00:03:01.955
And the second thing that will define me

00:03:01.955 --> 00:03:04.218
is what my 11 year old son thinks of me

00:03:04.218 --> 00:03:06.634
when he becomes a man.

00:03:06.634 --> 00:03:09.954
These are the two things
that define success for me,

00:03:09.954 --> 00:03:13.032
and in that is a little
bit of a conundrum,

00:03:13.032 --> 00:03:17.936
because I love what I do, it
is as I said my life's work,

00:03:17.936 --> 00:03:20.855
I love my son more than I love myself,

00:03:20.855 --> 00:03:23.351
and there is a constant tug.

00:03:23.351 --> 00:03:25.145
There isn't enough hours in the day

00:03:25.145 --> 00:03:27.662
to be able to do both extremely well,

00:03:27.662 --> 00:03:29.662
and so that is my challenge.

00:03:29.662 --> 00:03:33.079
There is nobody on planet Earth like you,

00:03:34.364 --> 00:03:38.338
and you are unique, you have unique gifts

00:03:38.338 --> 00:03:40.088
that nobody else has.

00:03:41.673 --> 00:03:45.048
So as quickly as you can accept that,

00:03:45.048 --> 00:03:47.048
the happier you will be.

00:03:48.217 --> 00:03:50.634
Why try and be somebody else?

00:03:51.995 --> 00:03:55.240
And why worry about others accepting you?

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.985
I am who I am, I'm proud of the fact

00:03:56.985 --> 00:03:58.568
that I am this way.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:00.927
And besides, I don't have a choice.

00:04:00.927 --> 00:04:02.162
(audience laughs)

00:04:02.162 --> 00:04:05.653
This is who I am, and
so I am very accepting

00:04:05.653 --> 00:04:08.327
of the fact that I come
with, and my wife is very

00:04:08.327 --> 00:04:10.911
accepting of the fact that I
come with a certain package,

00:04:10.911 --> 00:04:13.855
and it's taken me a
while to get comfortable

00:04:13.855 --> 00:04:17.542
with this is who I am
and these are the gifts

00:04:17.542 --> 00:04:19.883
that were given to me by my parents,

00:04:19.883 --> 00:04:23.641
and I'm not trying to be somebody else.

00:04:23.641 --> 00:04:26.308
I'm me and I'm happy about that.

00:04:28.743 --> 00:04:31.201
So I would say to you, you're unique,

00:04:31.201 --> 00:04:34.892
you have your own gifts,
there's nobody else like you

00:04:34.892 --> 00:04:38.098
and just get comfortable with that,

00:04:38.098 --> 00:04:39.775
and that will give you a sense of peace,

00:04:39.775 --> 00:04:42.193
and frankly, tremendous confidence

00:04:42.193 --> 00:04:43.571
because you're not trying
to please somebody else,

00:04:43.571 --> 00:04:44.591
you're trying to please yourself.

00:04:44.591 --> 00:04:47.199
Now it's easier said than done,

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:49.629
because we're trained from an early age

00:04:49.629 --> 00:04:51.848
to try and please others.

00:04:51.848 --> 00:04:53.359
I think that's a losing battle

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:56.539
because you ultimately end up
trying to be somebody else,

00:04:56.539 --> 00:04:58.885
and I say that about our firm as well.

00:04:58.885 --> 00:05:02.718
I mean, I say in the US
that we aspire to lead

00:05:03.932 --> 00:05:05.182
the profession.

00:05:06.633 --> 00:05:09.789
We're not defined or validated
by comparing ourselves

00:05:09.789 --> 00:05:11.601
to somebody else.

00:05:11.601 --> 00:05:13.015
We don't care.

00:05:13.015 --> 00:05:15.378
We don't care what PWC does.

00:05:15.378 --> 00:05:18.227
We don't care what McKinsey does.

00:05:18.227 --> 00:05:20.197
We don't care because we're
not going to be defined

00:05:20.197 --> 00:05:22.690
or validated by comparing
ourselves to them.

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:25.304
We're unique, we're
special, there's nobody else

00:05:25.304 --> 00:05:29.052
like us, so we're going
to define the profession

00:05:29.052 --> 00:05:30.135
in our image.

00:05:32.603 --> 00:05:35.141
And I think that applies
to individuals as well,

00:05:35.141 --> 00:05:39.178
we're all unique, and if you
try and be somebody else,

00:05:39.178 --> 00:05:41.498
I think you'll be quite unhappy.

00:05:41.498 --> 00:05:43.367
- I'm wondering if you
have any personal tricks

00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:45.873
on how you keep yourself motivated.

00:05:45.873 --> 00:05:48.956
- I set pretty high goals for myself,

00:05:49.792 --> 00:05:52.220
they're usually higher than
what I think most other

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:53.720
people set for me.

00:05:55.564 --> 00:05:59.064
And I just am very driven to achieve them.

00:06:03.676 --> 00:06:07.567
I'm a goal oriented
individual, and I just am

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:09.606
very driven to achieve those goals.

00:06:09.606 --> 00:06:13.606
- How do you motivate
people to get things done?

00:06:20.476 --> 00:06:23.764
- You know, the reason why
I paused was I was trying

00:06:23.764 --> 00:06:26.681
not to come up with a trite answer.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.717
I think, and this is an overused term,

00:06:34.049 --> 00:06:36.632
I think defining a overall goal

00:06:38.278 --> 00:06:42.445
that's reaching, in that
it isn't easily attainable,

00:06:44.195 --> 00:06:47.038
and then empowering, and
this is the overused word,

00:06:47.038 --> 00:06:49.675
and then empowering people to actually go

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:53.675
try and get it, and
ensuring that when they have

00:06:54.702 --> 00:06:58.510
a misstep that you're
there to help them along

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:00.306
is probably the way to.

00:07:00.306 --> 00:07:02.114
You know to be frank with you,

00:07:02.114 --> 00:07:03.497
most of the people that
we hire at Deloitte

00:07:03.497 --> 00:07:06.091
are extremely smart,
they're self motivated,

00:07:06.091 --> 00:07:09.841
they're very hard working,
and you've got to,

00:07:10.776 --> 00:07:12.481
much like a race horse,
you've got to make sure

00:07:12.481 --> 00:07:15.486
that you give them the space to develop

00:07:15.486 --> 00:07:17.653
and mature and accomplish.

00:07:19.487 --> 00:07:23.889
So I think most of my partners
would say the same thing,

00:07:23.889 --> 00:07:28.328
it takes an element of defining
the ultimate destination,

00:07:28.328 --> 00:07:32.166
then empowering people to
actually go to achieve it.

00:07:32.166 --> 00:07:34.523
You never make it by yourself,

00:07:34.523 --> 00:07:37.427
you always need others to help you.

00:07:37.427 --> 00:07:40.848
I'm good at what I do, I'm
really, really good at M&amp;A,

00:07:40.848 --> 00:07:43.492
and I'm not saying this
with any level of arrogance,

00:07:43.492 --> 00:07:44.811
the only reason why I'm saying that

00:07:44.811 --> 00:07:47.927
is I've put in over 25
years perfecting the craft.

00:07:47.927 --> 00:07:49.994
But one of the reasons why I'm good is

00:07:49.994 --> 00:07:52.524
that a partner out of the Dallas office

00:07:52.524 --> 00:07:55.574
took me under his wing and he said,

00:07:55.574 --> 00:07:58.125
let me apprentice you, let me show you how

00:07:58.125 --> 00:07:59.252
to become good at it.

00:07:59.252 --> 00:08:01.206
He didn't say it in exactly those words,

00:08:01.206 --> 00:08:04.284
but in retrospect, that's
what happened to me.

00:08:04.284 --> 00:08:06.988
So mentorship is incredibly important.

00:08:06.988 --> 00:08:09.079
If you want to be successful at something,

00:08:09.079 --> 00:08:10.574
you have to love it.

00:08:10.574 --> 00:08:12.287
And we're not fortunate enough

00:08:12.287 --> 00:08:15.083
that we know off the bat
that we love what we do.

00:08:15.083 --> 00:08:16.901
I didn't have a clue what I wanted to be,

00:08:16.901 --> 00:08:19.076
and so I was in that bucket of individuals

00:08:19.076 --> 00:08:21.217
that found myself in something,

00:08:21.217 --> 00:08:23.708
and then taught myself to love it,

00:08:23.708 --> 00:08:25.014
and so it becomes second nature to me

00:08:25.014 --> 00:08:27.940
and I really like what I do.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:31.748
And I think that's another
reason for being successful.

00:08:31.748 --> 00:08:34.088
As you stay in the consulting profession,

00:08:34.088 --> 00:08:37.198
you've got to get comfortable
with being uncomfortable.

00:08:37.198 --> 00:08:38.528
- [Interviewer] That's a good point, yeah.

00:08:38.528 --> 00:08:41.742
- It's a constant state of flux,

00:08:41.742 --> 00:08:46.100
my administrative assistant
kids me on a daily basis,

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:48.680
my schedule changes in terms
of where I'm going to be.

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:51.648
Every week is a different location

00:08:51.648 --> 00:08:53.782
and every project is very different

00:08:53.782 --> 00:08:55.574
in terms of the dynamics,
what you've got to achieve,

00:08:55.574 --> 00:08:59.024
the people issues, and so
at a certain point in time,

00:08:59.024 --> 00:09:01.892
you just become very comfortable with

00:09:01.892 --> 00:09:04.499
what would be termed as
stressful situations.

00:09:04.499 --> 00:09:06.399
As you go along on this journey,

00:09:06.399 --> 00:09:09.183
remember to stop and smell
the roses for a little bit.

00:09:09.183 --> 00:09:12.450
Frankly I didn't do enough
of that, I wish I had.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:14.320
I mean this is the funny thing about life,

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:16.227
you realize, you know
what I would love to do?

00:09:16.227 --> 00:09:18.695
I spoke to a number of students in India

00:09:18.695 --> 00:09:21.364
just a couple of days
ago, and I said to them,

00:09:21.364 --> 00:09:23.391
they said, in retrospect, what would you

00:09:23.391 --> 00:09:24.224
like to do?

00:09:24.224 --> 00:09:25.625
I said, well I'd like to be your age,

00:09:25.625 --> 00:09:28.082
which is 22, with the money I have now

00:09:28.082 --> 00:09:29.081
and the knowledge I have now.

00:09:29.081 --> 00:09:30.118
(audience laughs)

00:09:30.118 --> 00:09:32.522
But life is funny,
because you get the money

00:09:32.522 --> 00:09:35.249
and you get the knowledge,
but life has passed you by,

00:09:35.249 --> 00:09:39.249
and so my response to him
is, this is a journey,

00:09:40.115 --> 00:09:42.025
and take some time and smell the roses.

00:09:42.025 --> 00:09:42.858
(swooshes)

00:09:42.858 --> 00:09:43.969
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:09:43.969 --> 00:09:47.236
I made this video because
Aniket B asked me to,

00:09:47.236 --> 00:09:48.789
so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:09:48.789 --> 00:09:50.416
that you want me to profile next,

00:09:50.416 --> 00:09:51.552
leave it in the comments below

00:09:51.552 --> 00:09:52.658
and I'll see what I can do.

00:09:52.658 --> 00:09:54.372
I'd also love to know
which of the top 10 rules

00:09:54.372 --> 00:09:56.846
meant the most to you,
leave it in the comments,

00:09:56.846 --> 00:09:58.116
and I'll join the discussion.

00:09:58.116 --> 00:10:01.240
Thank you so much for
watching, continue to believe,

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:02.515
and we'll see you soon.

00:10:02.515 --> 00:10:04.765
(swooshes)

